<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Hanau-Münzenberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1458 CE – 1736 CE · capital Hanau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Main and Wetterau county of the counts of Hanau, whose welcome to Dutch and Walloon refugees in 1597 created the New Town of Hanau.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of Hanau, raised to counts in 1429, ruled lands around Hanau on the Main and had inherited part of the Münzenberg lordship in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetterau">Wetterau</a>. In 1458 the county was divided: the elder line kept Hanau-Münzenberg, while a younger line went on to found the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-hanau-lichtenberg">County of Hanau-Lichtenberg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-louis-ii-count-of-hanau-munzenberg">Philip Louis II</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> and in 1597 invited Reformed refugees from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a> to found the New Town of Hanau, which brought gold-working, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and other crafts. The county belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-rhenish-circle">Upper Rhenish Circle</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> Hanau withstood a long imperial siege, lifted in 1636.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Münzenberg line died out in 1642 and its lands passed to the Lichtenberg branch. When the last count, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-reinhard-iii-of-hanau">Johann Reinhard III</a>, died in 1736, Hanau-Münzenberg went to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse-kassel">Landgraviate of Hesse-Kassel</a>. Hanau later became known as the birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brothers-grimm">Brothers Grimm</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-hanau-munzenberg.jpg" alt="County of Hanau-Münzenberg" width="320" height="380"><figcaption>Image: ziegelbrenner · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanau-Muenzenberg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1458 CE – 1736 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hanau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip Louis II, Johann Reinhard III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hanau, Hanau-Münzenberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1597&amp;focus=county-of-hanau-munzenberg" aria-label="County of Hanau-Münzenberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4884 434 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Hanau-Münzenberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5204 690L5229 694M5204 690L5215 656M5204 690L5173 729"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5215 656h0M5173 729h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5204 690h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5204 690h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hanau. <a href="/atlas?year=1597&amp;focus=county-of-hanau-munzenberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
